{
  "subject": {
    "slug": "radiation-biology",
    "title": "Radiation Biology",
    "order": 7
  },
  "lesson": {
    "slug": "dna-damage",
    "title": "DNA Damage",
    "order": 0,
    "pages": [
      {
        "title": "DNA Is the Critical Target",
        "blocks": [
          {
            "type": "rich_text",
            "body": {
              "type": "doc",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": "While radiation can damage many cellular structures, nuclear DNA is considered the critical target for radiation-induced cell damage and death. A single ionizing track passing through the nucleus can break the sugar-phosphate backbone of the DNA double helix."
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "title": "Direct vs. Indirect Action",
        "blocks": [
          {
            "type": "rich_text",
            "body": {
              "type": "doc",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "callout",
                  "attrs": {
                    "kind": "key-principle"
                  },
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "paragraph",
                      "content": [
                        {
                          "type": "text",
                          "text": "Direct action:",
                          "marks": [
                            {
                              "type": "bold"
                            }
                          ]
                        },
                        {
                          "type": "text",
                          "text": " radiation directly ionizes the DNA molecule itself. More significant with high-LET radiation."
                        }
                      ]
                    },
                    {
                      "type": "paragraph",
                      "content": [
                        {
                          "type": "text",
                          "text": "Indirect action:",
                          "marks": [
                            {
                              "type": "bold"
                            }
                          ]
                        },
                        {
                          "type": "text",
                          "text": " radiation ionizes a nearby water molecule (cells are ~80% water), producing free radicals that then diffuse and damage DNA. This dominates with low-LET radiation like X-rays and gamma rays \u2014 which is exactly why oxygen (which helps \"fix\" free-radical damage) is such a potent radiosensitizer specifically for low-LET radiation."
                        }
                      ]
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "title": "Types of DNA Breaks",
        "blocks": [
          {
            "type": "rich_text",
            "body": {
              "type": "doc",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": "Radiation can break one or both strands of the DNA double helix:"
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "bulletList",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "listItem",
                      "content": [
                        {
                          "type": "paragraph",
                          "content": [
                            {
                              "type": "text",
                              "text": "Single-strand break",
                              "marks": [
                                {
                                  "type": "bold"
                                }
                              ]
                            },
                            {
                              "type": "text",
                              "text": " \u2014 usually repaired accurately using the intact complementary strand as a template"
                            }
                          ]
                        }
                      ]
                    },
                    {
                      "type": "listItem",
                      "content": [
                        {
                          "type": "paragraph",
                          "content": [
                            {
                              "type": "text",
                              "text": "Double-strand break",
                              "marks": [
                                {
                                  "type": "bold"
                                }
                              ]
                            },
                            {
                              "type": "text",
                              "text": " \u2014 far more consequential; both strands are severed at or near the same location, making accurate repair much harder"
                            }
                          ]
                        }
                      ]
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": "The frequency of single breaks (simple aberrations) increases as LET "
                    },
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": "decreases",
                      "marks": [
                        {
                          "type": "italic"
                        }
                      ]
                    },
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": ". This means low-LET radiations like X-rays and gamma rays produce a higher proportion of simple, versus complex multiple-break, aberrations."
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "title": "Chromosome Aberrations",
        "blocks": [
          {
            "type": "rich_text",
            "body": {
              "type": "doc",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": "When breaks occur in chromosomes, the broken ends can rejoin in ways that create visible structural abnormalities:"
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "callout",
                  "attrs": {
                    "kind": "key-principle"
                  },
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "paragraph",
                      "content": [
                        {
                          "type": "text",
                          "text": "If both arms of a chromosome are broken, the fragments can rejoin to form a "
                        },
                        {
                          "type": "text",
                          "text": "ring",
                          "marks": [
                            {
                              "type": "bold"
                            }
                          ]
                        },
                        {
                          "type": "text",
                          "text": ", leaving the remaining fragments joined together but without a centromere \u2014 an "
                        },
                        {
                          "type": "text",
                          "text": "acentric fragment",
                          "marks": [
                            {
                              "type": "bold"
                            }
                          ]
                        },
                        {
                          "type": "text",
                          "text": "."
                        }
                      ]
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": "Aberrations fall into two categories: "
                    },
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": "stable",
                      "marks": [
                        {
                          "type": "bold"
                        }
                      ]
                    },
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": " aberrations (like translocations or inversions) may cause genetic mix-up, but the cell's viability generally isn't affected. "
                    },
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": "Unstable",
                      "marks": [
                        {
                          "type": "bold"
                        }
                      ]
                    },
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": " aberrations (rings, dicentrics, anaphase bridges) usually kill the cell or prevent it from reproducing indefinitely."
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          },
          {
            "type": "knowledge_check",
            "key": "lq_page4_1",
            "stem": {
              "type": "doc",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": "A dicentric chromosome aberration is generally classified as:"
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            },
            "options": [
              "A stable aberration",
              "An unstable aberration"
            ],
            "answer": 1,
            "explanation": {
              "type": "doc",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": "Dicentrics, along with rings and anaphase bridges, are unstable aberrations that usually kill the cell or prevent indefinite reproduction."
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "title": "Damage Beyond the Nucleus",
        "blocks": [
          {
            "type": "rich_text",
            "body": {
              "type": "doc",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": "Although DNA is the critical target, other cellular structures are also damaged by ionizing radiation. The "
                    },
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": "plasma membrane",
                      "marks": [
                        {
                          "type": "bold"
                        }
                      ]
                    },
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": " can suffer structural damage to its phospholipid bilayer and proteins, altering what can pass in and out of the cell. Damage to "
                    },
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": "mitochondrial",
                      "marks": [
                        {
                          "type": "bold"
                        }
                      ]
                    },
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": " and "
                    },
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": "lysosomal",
                      "marks": [
                        {
                          "type": "bold"
                        }
                      ]
                    },
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": " membranes can also have severe consequences for the cell. Because the deposition of energy from secondary particles (electrons, protons, or alpha particles) is essentially random within matter, any site in the cell is potentially at risk."
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "title": "Wrap-Up",
        "blocks": [
          {
            "type": "rich_text",
            "body": {
              "type": "doc",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": "You've covered why DNA is the critical target, direct vs. indirect action (and why the distinction matters for the oxygen effect), single- vs. double-strand breaks, chromosome aberrations (stable vs. unstable, ring/acentric fragment formation), and damage to non-nuclear cell structures."
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "paragraph",
                  "content": [
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "text": "This is the molecular-level foundation underneath everything covered in Cell Survival Curves and Cell Sensitivity \u2014 DNA damage is the actual mechanism behind the survival curve shapes and radiosensitivity patterns in those lessons."
                    }
                  ]
                }
              ]
            }
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  }
}
